@meta
id: 27-02
title: Dials, not switches
chapter: 27
type: lesson
skills: set-option
par: 32
minutes: 4
vscode: Settings, "Editor: Tab Size", type 4
open: schema.sql

@brief
Not every option is a yes or a no. Plenty of them hold a number or a
string, and those take an `=` and a value.

`shiftwidth` is a number: it is how far `>>` and `<<` move a line, and how
much indent `o` gives you inside a block. Your setup has it at 2, which is
right for the TypeScript in this repo.

This is not TypeScript. The SQL in `schema.sql` follows the database
team's convention of four spaces inside a `CREATE TABLE`. Set the dial to
4 for this session and indent the four column lines.

No spaces around the `=`, and no quotes around the value. Vim's command
line is not a shell.

@keys
| `:set shiftwidth=4` | a number option takes `=` and a value |
| `:set shiftwidth?` | what is it right now |
| `:set sw=4` | the short name, for live use |
| `:set sw=4 et` | several options in one command, separated by spaces |

@hints
`>>` moves a line by `shiftwidth`. You do not need a different indent
command, you need a different `shiftwidth`.
--
Number options are set with `=`. `:set shiftwidth=` and the number.
--
`:set shiftwidth=4` then Enter, then `2G` and `4>>`.

@solution
    :set shiftwidth=4<Enter>  2G  4>>  :wq<Enter>

Twenty-seven keystrokes.

The syntax rules for values are short and worth knowing exactly, because
vim will not warn you when you get them wrong — it will usually just
throw an error you then have to read:

- No spaces around `=`. `:set sw = 4` is three separate arguments and an
  error.
- No quotes. `:set backupdir='/tmp'` puts literal quote characters in the
  path.
- A space inside a string value has to be escaped with a backslash:
  `:set makeprg=npm\ run\ build`.
- Several options can share one `:set`: `:set sw=4 ts=4 et` sets three.

If you want to know what a number option is holding, `:set sw?` tells you.
If you want to know what *every* option is holding, `:options` opens a
browsable window of the lot, grouped by subject, with each option's value
and one line of description. It is slow to read and occasionally exactly
what you want when you know the behaviour you are chasing but not the
name.

@start schema.sql
CREATE TABLE orders (
id CHAR(26) NOT NULL,
customer_id CHAR(26) NOT NULL,
total_pence INTEGER NOT NULL,
PRIMARY KEY (id)
);

@expected schema.sql
CREATE TABLE orders (
    id CHAR(26) NOT NULL,
    customer_id CHAR(26) NOT NULL,
    total_pence INTEGER NOT NULL,
    PRIMARY KEY (id)
);
